<article class="env-corollary" id="0569">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0569">Corollary <span data-tag="0569">3.5.9.11</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer and suppose we are given a homotopy pullback diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation" id="056A">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="056A" href="/tag/056A">3.79</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:homotopy-pullback-square-truncatedness} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f} &amp;  X' \ar [d]^{f'} \\ Y \ar [r]^-{g} &amp;  Y'. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> If $f'$ is $n$-truncated, then $f$ is also $n$-truncated. The converse holds if $\pi _0(g)$ is surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using Proposition <a data-tag="00UV" href="/tag/00UV">3.1.8.1</a>, we can reduce to the case where $f$ and $f'$ are Kan fibrations. In this case, our assumption that (<a data-tag="056A" href="/tag/056A">3.79</a>) is a homotopy pullback square guarantees that for each vertex $y \in Y$, the induced map of fibers $X_{y} \rightarrow X'_{g(y)}$ is a homotopy equivalence (Example <a data-tag="010S" href="/tag/010S">3.4.1.4</a>) In particular, $X_{y}$ is $n$-truncated if and only if $X'_{g(y)}$ is $n$-truncated (Corollary <a data-tag="0550" href="/tag/0550">3.5.7.8</a>). The desired result now follows from the criterion of Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> (together with Remark <a data-tag="0567" href="/tag/0567">3.5.9.9</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
